WEBVTT

00:06.630 --> 00:12.240
Your PMI exam there are two terms you need to be familiar with the project management lifecycle and

00:12.240 --> 00:21.300
the project lifecycle the project management lifecycle describes the initiation the planning the executing

00:21.390 --> 00:24.020
monitoring controlling and closing.

00:24.020 --> 00:32.460
It's the common process groups that we all use this project managers the project lifecycle though is

00:32.460 --> 00:35.790
unique to each type of project.

00:35.790 --> 00:39.030
So it describes the phases of the project.

00:39.030 --> 00:46.530
So if you think about in construction that we have the pre-build phase the permits and the surveys and

00:46.530 --> 00:53.100
all the paperwork's the and so on that we have the foundation phase in framing and electrical and plumbing

00:53.150 --> 01:01.770
and finishing that each Beys makes up that project lifecycle the project management lifecycle is always

01:01.770 --> 01:05.820
initiating planning executing monitor controlling and closing.

01:05.940 --> 01:13.640
So we talked about application areas a moment ago the project lifecycle is unique to the type of work

01:13.640 --> 01:14.660
you're doing.

01:14.720 --> 01:20.090
In other words we won't have the same project lifecycle in a manufacturing project as an I.T. project

01:20.120 --> 01:26.510
as a construction project that those are unique to that application area to the type of work that's

01:26.510 --> 01:27.720
being done.

01:27.740 --> 01:34.730
In fact some projects may have their own unique lifecycle as well their own unique phases.

01:34.820 --> 01:42.740
So you might not always have a framing phase or a plumbing phase or whatever the case may be on a construction

01:42.740 --> 01:47.960
project because maybe you are framing your remodeling or doing a drywall or whatever the case may be

01:48.230 --> 01:51.300
in your construction project.

01:51.320 --> 01:57.620
The point I'm trying to make is that the project lifecycle is always unique.

01:57.650 --> 02:03.930
Each phase describes the work and the deliverable that that phase is creating.

02:03.980 --> 02:09.690
So if we have a phase called Foundation we all can relate to well that's creating the foundation in

02:09.690 --> 02:16.010
this phase or if we have a phase called framing we're talking about the skeleton of the house the framing

02:16.010 --> 02:17.190
the structure.

02:17.240 --> 02:23.100
So it's just a quick way of describing what type of work takes place in that phase.

02:23.250 --> 02:26.660
And then at the end of that phase creates that deliverable.

02:26.660 --> 02:32.930
So we're going to see this concept of phases a lot that the end of one phase allows the next phase to

02:32.930 --> 02:38.280
begin and the end of a phase often creates a milestone.

02:38.510 --> 02:42.300
So a milestone is a significant accomplishment in the project.

02:42.320 --> 02:47.280
So the end of the foundation phase would create a milestone of the foundation.

02:47.630 --> 02:51.840
So it describes what works taking place and what does it create.

02:52.210 --> 02:55.430
All right so that's the project lifecycle.

02:55.430 --> 02:55.690
All right.

02:55.700 --> 02:56.540
Good job.

02:56.630 --> 02:59.790
Keep moving forward.
